/* sf-icons.js - small, well-drawn pictures for StudyFlow's diagrams.

   WHY THIS EXISTS. The diagrams a lesson falls back on - steps, a cycle, the
   ideas around a topic, a timeline - were boxes with the words inside, and
   they looked it. A model asked to draw a sun draws a circle; asked to draw a
   cloud, a lumpy ellipse. Neither problem is fixed by prompting: the first is
   a layout with nothing to show, the second a hand that cannot draw. These are
   pictures someone could draw, ready to place:

     SFIcons.markup(name, cx, cy, size, ink, tint)   a <g> centred on cx,cy
     SFIcons.match(text, subject)                    the icon a label names, or ''
     SFIcons.pick(labels, subject)                   one per label, repeats avoided
     SFIcons.has(name), SFIcons.names()

   The diagram kit puts one beside each step or idea it can name. SFSceneKit
   lets a model drawing place one ({"s":"icon","name":"sun",...}) for the
   things AROUND the subject - the sun over a leaf, a person at a lever - while
   the subject itself is still drawn.

   TRUTH FIRST. An icon is a claim that the label is about that thing, so the
   matcher only answers when a word in the label names it: "condenses into
   clouds" gets a cloud, "nucleus" gets nothing (an atom would be the wrong
   nucleus). No match is a normal answer; the caller draws a number or a
   letter instead. Words are matched whole, or as a stem where the rule says
   so ("evaporat*"), never as a substring of another word.

   Every icon is two shapes in a 256 box: a pale layer and an outline. No ids,
   no gradients, nothing that can collide with another picture on the page.

   ICONS: Phosphor Icons (duotone), https://phosphoricons.com
   Copyright (c) 2023 Phosphor Icons, MIT License - permission is granted to
   use, copy, modify and distribute, provided this notice is kept. Coordinates
   rounded to 0.1 of 256, which is invisible at the sizes drawn here.
   cell, lungs and volcano are StudyFlow's own, drawn to match. */
